   "columns": [
    [
     {
      "type": "textmedia",
      "header": "<h1> FAQ </h1>",
      "body": "<p>Frequently asked questions</p>",
      "images": [],
      "videos": [],
      "pos": "text",
      "cols": 1,
      "valign": "top",
      "ftype": "text",
      "id": "c164677",
      "bg": "",
      "space": {
       "before": "default",
       "after": "default"
      }
     }
    ],
    [
     {
      "type": "accordion",
      "header": "",
      "items": [
       {
        "title": "How do I know when a diamond tool should be replaced?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>To know when to replace a diamond-coated tool, use a tool inspection machine for a µ‑precise condition analysis. A tool that needs to be replaced will have profiles that no longer fit together correctly and create lines on the finished product. </p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164676",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "What causes a diamond tool to stop cutting effectively?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>The leading cause of a diamond tool no longer cutting effectively is incorrect tool setups, which causes uneven wear and mismatch of the profiles. This leads to premature wear, heat buildup, and distortion of the tool, thus creating poor results on the finished product and damage to the tools.</p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164679",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "How accurately can a ZOLLER system measure tools?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>A ZOLLER tool inspection machine can measure with µ‑precision. </p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164681",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "How do we standardize tool inspections across operators?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>The easiest way to standardize tool inspections is to invest in a tool inspection machine, which can provide precise results in seconds. The ZOLLER »conDiaZ« provides easily reproduced, operator-independent measurement results. </p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164683",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "What are the limitations of the »conDiaZ«?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>The »conDiaZ«does not support HSK Tooling and some long tool holders. </p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164685",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "Is the »conDiaZ« capable of sending tool offsets to the CNC machine?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>Yes, the »conDiaZ« capable of sending tool offsets to the CNC machine.</p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164691",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "Can the »conDiaZ« measure polishing tools?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>Yes, the »conDiaZ« can measure polishing tools. </p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164692",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "How often should I measure my tools?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>For optimum performance and tool longevity, sink tools should be sharpened and measured every 300–500 linear feet, while larger wheels should be sharpened every 500-700 linear feet. </p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164693",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "Can presetting tools improve tool life?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<p>Yes, presetting tools can improve tool life. On average, we see a 40–50% increase in tool life by presetting and sharpening tools. </p>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164694",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       },
       {
        "title": "How does presetting and sharpening tools save money?",
        "blocks": [
         {
          "type": "textmedia",
          "header": "",
          "body": "<ul><li><p>Presetting and sharpening extends tool life by 40-50% </p></li><li><p>Without a presetter, a simple profile takes 2-3 hours (plus the time it takes to run a test piece), while a complex profile can take 6+ hours. With a presetter, it only takes fifteen minutes. Considering machine uptime in the United States is approximately $500 per hour, this is a significant cost savings. </p></li><li><p>If the tools are perfectly set up and consuming the correct stock removal values, then you can attain faster CNC rates. </p></li><li><p>Tools that are set perfectly will produce pieces that require minimal to no hand polishing, thus reducing labor costs.</p></li></ul>",
          "images": [],
          "videos": [],
          "pos": "text",
          "cols": 1,
          "valign": "top",
          "ftype": "text",
          "id": "c164695",
          "bg": "",
          "space": {
           "before": "default",
           "after": "default"
          }
         }
        ]
       }
      ],
      "ftype": "accordion",
